# timing-terms — the constants this repo cannot compute, in one place.
#
# ── Why a data file and not a copy in every script that needs one ──────────
# Most timing constants here are READ FROM THE CODE THAT USES THEM — the
# power-cut budget out of the policy script, a unit's stop budget out of the
# unit. Those cannot drift, because there is nothing to drift FROM: the reader
# and the user of the value are the same file.
#
# Two classes of constant do not work that way:
#
#   MEASURED   — how long the hardware actually takes. Not derivable from any
#                source in this tree. It comes from a stopwatch.
#   FIRMWARE   — compiled into the controller's AVR binary. Not in this repo,
#                not on the node, and not readable over I2C.
#
# Pasted into whichever script needs them, those go stale in one copy and not
# the other, with at best a comment promising the two will be compared. A
# promise is not a mechanism. So: ONE file, read by the offline tests, read by
# timing-windows, and shipped to the node verbatim so the node reads the same
# bytes. timing-windows prints this file's checksum, so two runs that show the
# same number provably used the same terms.
#
# ── When to edit this file ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# * You re-measured something on hardware. Put the NEW number here and say how
#   you got it. Do not "adjust" a measured term to make an assertion pass: a
#   changed system should force a RE-MEASUREMENT, not a recomputation.
# * You reflashed the controller with modified firmware. Update the FW_ block
#   AND bump FW_REV, or every guard that depends on the revision is defeated —
#   see the warning in that section.
#
# Format: KEY=VALUE, one per line, no quoting, no expansion. Parsed by grep,
# never sourced: this file ships to the node, and a report that executes its
# own input is a report you cannot trust on a machine you are debugging.

# ── MEASURED ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# These two describe YOUR IMAGE, not the Witty Pi, and the values below are
# from a reference setup: a Pi Zero W with a Witty Pi 4, an uncompressed
# image. They are the right order of magnitude for a small Pi and they are not
# a fact about your build. Re-measure them on the image you actually ship
# before trusting the margins computed from them.

# Power-on -> the point your image considers "booted".
# Capture the console from BEFORE a scheduled wake so its first byte is the
# bootloader banner, then anchor to /proc/stat btime. The reference breakdown:
# 2.5 s firmware+bootloader, 7.5 s bootloader->kernel, 95.9 s kernel->userspace
# gate. Measured 105.9, carried as 106.
T_BOOT=106

# Power-on -> the point your image marks this boot healthy.
#
# Only meaningful if your image has such a step — an A/B scheme returning a
# boot attempt, a health-check unit, a watchdog handing over. wittypi-schedule
# uses it as a floor so a duty cycle can never shut the node down before that
# step has had time to run. If nothing in your image marks a boot good, this is
# simply a conservative lower bound on the awake window.
#
# This is NOT T_BOOT plus the health soak. A soak that runs until a fixed
# uptime SUBSUMES the boot rather than following it; adding the two gives ~400
# here and is wrong by 90 seconds. Measure from power-on, not from boot.
T_MARKGOOD=312

# Halt request -> the kernel's "reboot: Power down", from a console capture of
# a real `systemctl poweroff`. In the reference capture: 39.279 s (kernel's
# "reboot: Power down") minus 31.257 s (first unit-stop message) = 8.022 s,
# carried as 8.
#
# That run includes a FULL wake-guard arm, not its fast recheck. With alarm1
# cleared beforehand, wake-guard.service's ExecStop takes the slow "nothing
# armed yet" path — 4.004 s in the same capture, close to its TimeoutStopSec=5
# ceiling. On the controller-initiated path the daemon's own wake-guard call
# usually arms first, so the unit's ExecStop hits the fast already-armed
# recheck (~0.7 s). But a Linux-initiated shutdown — a human typing `poweroff`,
# or anything reaching wake-guard.service with nothing pre-armed — pays the
# slow path and nothing else reserves budget for it. That is a real code path,
# not a contrived worst case, so it is what this constant carries.
#
# Shutdown mechanics alone, subtracting that arm: 8.022 - 4.018 = ~4 s. Most of
# the cost is the arming, not the rest of the shutdown.
#
# One measurement of a CLEAN shutdown. A node with more dirty pages, a unit
# slow to stop, or a tired card takes longer, and the power-cut budget does not
# stretch. Treat the slack over this number as the real safety margin, not the
# number itself.
T_SHUTDOWN=8

# ── FIRMWARE — Witty Pi 4, ATtiny841 ───────────────────────────────────────
# Read from WittyPi4.ino. Every counter here is a 1 Hz watchdog tick
# (`WDTCSR |= 6; // trigger every second`, :309), so a "count of 120" is 120
# seconds of wall clock.
#
# These are only true for one firmware build. If you recompile and flash the
# controller, these numbers may no longer describe the hardware — and the node
# has no way to know that, because the only thing it can read is the revision
# register. So:
#
#   1. change a constant in the firmware  ->  change it HERE, and
#   2. BUMP I2C_FW_REVISION in the firmware AND FW_REV below.
#
# If you skip step 2 the revision check passes against modified firmware and
# every guard downstream of it is silently reasoning about the wrong hardware.
# A stock rev-7 controller and your recompiled one are indistinguishable over
# I2C unless you make them distinguishable.

# What `i2cReg[I2C_FW_REVISION]` is set to (:270). Compared against register 12
# on the board; a mismatch is a hazard, not a footnote.
FW_REV=7

# The alarm coincidence window (:878, :890).
#
# Not a timeout. The controller reduces both the RTC and the alarm to
# date*86400 + h*3600 + m*60 + s and fires only while 0 <= now - alarm < this,
# tested at 1 Hz. A missed alarm does not fire LATE; it does not fire at all,
# until guaranteed wake. Everything about UTC-everywhere follows from the fact
# that a DST conversion is a 3600 s step against a 2 s tolerance.
FW_ALARM_WINDOW=2

# Temperature actions are suppressed for this long after power-on
# (`skipTempShutdownCount < 120`, :927; gate at :982; reset in powerOn(), :448).
#
# The reason register is still written during the inhibit. :935 sets
# I2C_ACTION_REASON = REASON_OVER_TEMPERATURE and only THEN calls
# turnOffIfPowerOn(), which is the part the counter gates. So register 11 can
# read 6 on a node that did not shut down for temperature — do not treat a
# reason of 6/7 as proof that the last power-off was thermal.
FW_THERMAL_INHIBIT=120

# Retry interval when alarm1 fires while the rail is already up (:692).
FW_ALARM1_RETRY=4

# Factory default for register 18, the housekeeping pulse while asleep (:274).
# Our policy does not set this register, so the factory value is what runs.
FW_PULSE_INTERVAL=4
